+/// The longest value an `orphan:` mark may hold, in bytes — the bound [`decode_orphan_mark`]
+/// holds every stored value to before it reads one.
+///
+/// Every shape a writer spells is far inside it. A legacy mark is at most 20 digits; a structured
+/// one is under 80 bytes of JSON around its unreference-event identity, and the longest identity
+/// this protocol mints — a per-part session retirement token,
+/// `s:<upload-id>:<epoch>:<part-number>:<attempt-id>` — is 95 bytes. The rest is room for an
+/// identity a later writer mints (a move's nonce, `0016:1177-1187`).
+///
+/// It is a bound at all because of what a pass does with a mark it can read: GC's reclaim-intent
+/// commit carries the value it read as a precondition and the value's reclaiming form as a put,
+/// for up to a thousand marks at once. Marks of unbounded length would make that commit
+/// unbounded, and a batch of marks near the backend's value limit would not fit one transaction —
+/// failing every pass that met them. So the bound is on the **longest form a mark takes** — its
+/// reclaiming form, which is a structured mark's value plus 18 bytes: a mark GC can read is one
+/// whose reclaiming form GC can write and read back. Anything longer matches none of the three
+/// shapes, which fails closed.
+pub const MAX_ORPHAN_MARK_LEN: usize = 512;
+
+/// An `orphan:` mark's **value**: the instant its fragment became orphaned, the identity of the
+/// unreference event that wrote it when the writer recorded one, and whether GC has already
+/// decided to reclaim the fragment (proposal 0016, `0016:1189-1204`, `:1321-1336`).
+///
+/// Three shapes, told apart on the wire by JSON type:
+///
+/// | Shape | Bytes | Written by |
+/// |---|---|---|
+/// | **legacy** | a bare canonical decimal, `1700000000000` | every writer that predates 0016 — [`unlink`], the overwrite paths, the post-restore pass, reconstruction, rebalance, the custodian's `mark_orphaned` |
+/// | **structured** | `{"orphaned_at_millis":…,"event":"…"}` | an unreference event that names itself — a retirement drain, whose `event` is its [`crate::multipart::RetireToken`]'s canonical string |
+/// | **reclaiming** | `{"orphaned_at_millis":…,"event":"…","reclaiming":true}`, `event` omitted when the mark it replaced carried none | GC, recording that it is about to reclaim the fragment — before it does |
+///
+/// A legacy mark carries **no** event identity; a structured one always does. The reclaiming
+/// shape keeps whatever the mark it replaced carried — the stamp its grace window was measured
+/// from and the identity, if any — so [`Self::reclaiming`] of either other shape is one. It is
+/// terminal: the next pass that meets it finishes the reclamation with no second grace test
+/// (`0016:1321-1333`).
+///
+/// # One spelling per value
+///
+/// [`decode_orphan_mark`] accepts a value only when it is exactly the bytes [`Self::encode`] writes
+/// for what it decodes to. Two things follow. A legacy value re-encodes as the same legacy value,
+/// so a pass that merely reads the ledger never rewrites a mark and never restarts its grace
+/// clock (the `already_marked` property, `0016:1208-1211`). And a reader holding a decoded mark
+/// holds the exact bytes it was read from — the precondition an exact-value CAS on the mark needs
+/// — without keeping a copy of them.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct OrphanMark {
+    orphaned_at_millis: u64,
+    event: Option<String>,
+    reclaiming: bool,
+}
+
+impl OrphanMark {
+    /// A mark stamped `orphaned_at_millis` that names no unreference event — the **legacy**
+    /// shape, byte for byte what every writer that predates 0016 puts.
+    pub const fn legacy(orphaned_at_millis: u64) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            orphaned_at_millis,
+            event: None,
+            reclaiming: false,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// A mark stamped `orphaned_at_millis` by the unreference event `event` — the **structured**
+    /// shape. For a retirement drain `event` is its [`crate::multipart::RetireToken`]'s canonical
+    /// string, which is what lets a reader find the `retire:bytes:` obligation still draining for
+    /// it with one keyed read (`0016:1242-1247`).
+    ///
+    /// Refused, as [`OrphanMarkError::TooLong`], when an identity that long would make a mark
+    /// [`decode_orphan_mark`] refuses: no mark exists that its own reader cannot read.
+    pub fn for_event(
+        orphaned_at_millis: u64,
+        event: impl Into<String>,
+    ) -> std::result::Result<Self, OrphanMarkError> {
+        let mark = Self {
+            orphaned_at_millis,
+            event: Some(event.into()),
+            reclaiming: false,
+        };
+        mark.checked_len()?;
+        Ok(mark)
+    }
+
+    /// The length bound, applied to the longest form this mark takes — its reclaiming form
+    /// (see [`MAX_ORPHAN_MARK_LEN`]).
+    fn checked_len(&self) -> std::result::Result<(), OrphanMarkError> {
+        let len = self.reclaiming().encode().len();
+        if len > MAX_ORPHAN_MARK_LEN {
+            return Err(OrphanMarkError::TooLong { len });
+        }
+        Ok(())
+    }
+
+    /// This mark with GC's reclaim decision recorded — the **reclaiming** shape, keeping this
+    /// mark's stamp and identity. Idempotent.
+    pub fn reclaiming(&self) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            reclaiming: true,
+            ..self.clone()
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The instant the fragment became orphaned (logical milliseconds) — the start of its grace
+    /// window.
+    pub const fn orphaned_at_millis(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.orphaned_at_millis
+    }
+
+    /// The unreference event that wrote the mark, when it named itself. `None` for every legacy
+    /// mark.
+    pub fn event(&self) -> Option<&str> {
+        self.event.as_deref()
+    }
+
+    /// Whether GC has already decided to reclaim the fragment.
+    pub const fn is_reclaiming(&self) -> bool {
+        self.reclaiming
+    }
+
+    /// The stored bytes: the bare decimal for a legacy mark, the closed JSON object otherwise —
+    /// with an absent `event` and a `false` `reclaiming` omitted, never spelled out.
+    pub fn encode(&self) -> Vec<u8> {
+        if self.event.is_none() && !self.reclaiming {
+            return self.orphaned_at_millis.to_string().into_bytes();
+        }
+        encode(&OrphanMarkWire {
+            orphaned_at_millis: self.orphaned_at_millis,
+            event: self.event.clone(),
+            reclaiming: self.reclaiming,
+        })
+        .to_vec()
+    }
+}
+
+/// The wire shape of a **structured** or **reclaiming** [`OrphanMark`]: closed, and with both
+/// optional fields omitted when absent — never `"event":null`, never `"reclaiming":false` — so the
+/// encoder's image is exactly the set [`decode_orphan_mark`] accepts.
+#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
+#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
+struct OrphanMarkWire {
+    orphaned_at_millis: u64,
+    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
+    event: Option<String>,
+    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "is_false")]
+    reclaiming: bool,
+}
+
+fn is_false(flag: &bool) -> bool {
+    !*flag
+}
+
+/// Why an `orphan:` mark's value decodes as none of [`OrphanMark`]'s three shapes.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum OrphanMarkError {
+    /// Longer than [`MAX_ORPHAN_MARK_LEN`] — as stored, or once in its reclaiming form.
+    TooLong {
+        /// The length found over the bound.
+        len: usize,
+    },
+    /// Neither a canonical decimal instant nor a JSON object — text, a torn value, or a number
+    /// spelled with a sign or a leading zero.
+    Unrecognized,
+    /// A JSON object that is not a mark: a field missing, mistyped or unknown.
+    Malformed {
+        /// What the decoder refused.
+        detail: String,
+    },
+    /// A JSON object that names no event and is not reclaiming. A mark without an event is
+    /// spelled as the bare decimal, so this is a second spelling of a legacy mark.
+    EventMissing,
+    /// A mark spelled other than its one spelling: whitespace, a reordered field, an
+    /// `"event":null` or a `"reclaiming":false`.
+    Noncanonical,
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for OrphanMarkError {
+    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        match self {
+            Self::TooLong { len } => write!(
+                f,
+                "orphan mark value takes {len} bytes (as stored or in its reclaiming form), past \
+                 the {MAX_ORPHAN_MARK_LEN}-byte bound"
+            ),
+            Self::Unrecognized => {
+                f.write_str("orphan mark value is neither a canonical decimal nor a JSON object")
+            }
+            Self::Malformed { detail } => write!(f, "orphan mark value is malformed: {detail}"),
+            Self::EventMissing => f.write_str(
+                "orphan mark value is an object naming no event and not reclaiming (a mark \
+                 without an event is spelled as the bare decimal)",
+            ),
+            Self::Noncanonical => {
+                f.write_str("orphan mark value is not spelled the one way its writer spells it")
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+impl std::error::Error for OrphanMarkError {}
+
+/// Decode an `orphan:` mark's value into one of its three shapes ([`OrphanMark`]), or say why it
+/// is none of them.
+///
+/// **Dual-format** (`0016:1198-1201`, ADR-0045): a canonical decimal is a legacy mark and keeps its
+/// meaning exactly — its instant, no event; a JSON object is a structured or reclaiming mark.
+/// Anything else is an error, never a value: a pass that meets one must act on nothing — keep the
+/// fragment, leave the value exactly as it is, and name it to an operator — because a mark it
+/// cannot read is still evidence that something stranded the fragment, and the instant it would
+/// have given is unknown.
+///
+/// **Exact**: the value must be the very bytes [`OrphanMark::encode`] writes for what it decodes to
+/// (see [`OrphanMark`]'s "One spelling per value"). **Bounded**: it and its reclaiming form must
+/// fit [`MAX_ORPHAN_MARK_LEN`].
+pub fn decode_orphan_mark(value: &[u8]) -> std::result::Result<OrphanMark, OrphanMarkError> {
+    // Before any parse, so no stored value makes a reader do work in proportion to its length.
+    if value.len() > MAX_ORPHAN_MARK_LEN {
+        return Err(OrphanMarkError::TooLong { len: value.len() });
+    }
+    let mark = if value.first() == Some(&b'{') {
+        let wire: OrphanMarkWire = decode(value).map_err(|err| OrphanMarkError::Malformed {
+            detail: err.to_string(),
+        })?;
+        if wire.event.is_none() && !wire.reclaiming {
+            return Err(OrphanMarkError::EventMissing);
+        }
+        OrphanMark {
+            orphaned_at_millis: wire.orphaned_at_millis,
+            event: wire.event,
+            reclaiming: wire.reclaiming,
+        }
+    } else {
+        let at = std::str::from_utf8(value)
+            .ok()
+            .and_then(parse_canonical_u64)
+            .ok_or(OrphanMarkError::Unrecognized)?;
+        OrphanMark::legacy(at)
+    };
+    if mark.encode() != value {
+        return Err(OrphanMarkError::Noncanonical);
+    }
+    mark.checked_len()?;
+    Ok(mark)
+}
