+// ---- property 13: staged protection and reclaim-before-destroy under concurrent writers (#662) ----
+//
+// Proposal 0016 gives GC two paths whose soundness is about interleavings, and each is swept
+// here with a genuinely concurrent task over the simulated-TiKV model, where every read and
+// commit spans a simulated network hop:
+//
+// * the STAGED BUILD (`gc::referenced_fragments`, decision 2). A multipart chunk's protection
+//   moves from its owned `sidx:` entry to a `part:` record when its part commits, and from the
+//   `part:` record to a committed inode when the upload publishes. The build reads each handoff's
+//   source before its destination (`0016:782-800`), so wherever a handoff lands the chunk is seen
+//   in at least one class. `crates/custodian/tests/staged_protection.rs` scripts one landing per
+//   handoff; here the seed picks when each lands.
+// * the RECLAIM INTENT (`0016:1312-1336`). GC compare-and-sets a mark into its `reclaiming` shape
+//   before it deletes the fragment, so a move's adoption CAS preconditioned on the mark's earlier
+//   bytes loses from the instant reclamation begins. Here a mover adopts a pre-marked fragment —
+//   publishing a placement that names it and deleting the pre-mark, in one CAS — at an instant
+//   the seed picks, while GC reclaims the same fragment through a delete that spans a hop, as a
+//   real delete RPC does.
+//
+// The properties are the invariant itself: no staged or committed fragment is ever deleted, and
+// no committed placement ever names a deleted fragment — while a fragment the mover did not adopt
+// is still reclaimed. Landing points are drawn in half-milliseconds, so a landing falls strictly
+// between two of the pass's one-millisecond reads rather than tied with one; each property has a
+// coverage property proving the landing it is about is reached.
